      "problem": "Factor $405-5{x}^{4}$ completely.",
      "worked_solution": "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms in our expression have a common factor of $5.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n405-5{x}^{4}=5\\cdot 81-5\\cdot {x}^{4}=5(81-{x}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n5(81-{x}^{4})=5({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n5({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})=5(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2}) & =5(9+{x}^{2})({3}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
