      "problem": "Factor the expression $q-q{p}^{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $q.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $q,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nq-q{p}^{4}=q(1-{p}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parenthesis can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\nq(1-{p}^{4})=q({1}^{2}-{({p}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\nq({1}^{2}-{({p}^{2})}^{2})=q(1+{p}^{2})(1-{p}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1+{p}^{2})(1-{p}^{2}) & =q(1+{p}^{2})({1}^{2}-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =q(1+{p}^{2})(1+p)(1-p)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
